<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Using Bivariate Data in Context</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00775</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Using Bivariate Data in Context</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2f9cf4dd-9506-48d1-9b30-d5b696ec4468</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">In the following activity, you will complete a series of questions regarding scatter
        plots and the variables that represent the data. </para>
      <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00002">Log into <link url="https://student.desmos.com">student.desmos.com</link> using the
        information provided by your teacher to complete the activity.</para>
      </note>

      <para id="para-00003">Examine the unlabeled graphs below, then match the variables to the scatter plot you think they best fit. Choose from among the following variable combinations. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.
        <table class="unnumbered" id="table-00001" summary="This table presents examples of paired X and Y variables, suitable for exploring different types of relationships or statistical analysis.">
          <tgroup cols="1">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = average number of free throws shot in a season<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = basketball team score per game
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = daily low temperature in Celsius for Denver, CO<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = boxes of cereal in stock at a grocery in Miami, FL
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = measured student height in feet<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = measured student height in inches
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = average number of minutes spent in a waiting room<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = hospital satisfaction rating
                </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
      </para>

      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">1. </span>Which variables best describe the relationship represented in the graph below?</para>
          <media alt="A scatter plot on a coordinate plane with an x-axis ranging from 0 to 33 in increments of 3, and a y-axis ranging from 0 to 130 in increments of 10. A cluster of data points is located roughly between the x values of 12 and 24, and the y values of 80 and 120.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_01_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005"><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = daily low temperature in Celsius for Denver, CO<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = boxes of cereal in stock at a grocery in Miami, FL</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">2. </span>Which variables best describe the relationship represented in the graph below?</para>
          <media alt="A scatter plot on a coordinate plane. The x-axis is labeled x and ranges from 0 to 33 in increments of 3. The y-axis is labeled y and ranges from 0 to 130 in increments of 10. A set of black data points shows an upward trend, with values roughly between x equals 14 and x equals 25, and y equals 80 and y equals 120.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_01_02.svg"/>
          </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007"><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = average number of free throws shot in a season<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = basketball team score per game</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">3. </span>Which variables best describe the relationship represented in the graph below?</para>
          <media alt="A scatter plot on a coordinate plane with an x-axis labeled from 1 to 8 and a y-axis labeled from 0 to 80 in increments of 10. A series of black dots shows a strong positive linear trend, starting near the point where x equals 5 and y equals 60 and moving upward toward approximately x equals 6.5 and y equals 75.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_01_03.svg"/>
          </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">
            <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = measured student height in feet<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = measured student height in inches
          </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00010" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">4. </span>Which variables best describe the relationship represented in the graph below?</para>
          <media alt="A scatter plot with the x-axis ranging from 0 to 135 and the y-axis ranging from 0 to 10. The plotted points are widely scattered but display a general, weak negative correlation.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_01_04.svg"/>
          </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = average number of minutes spent in a waiting room<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = hospital satisfaction rating</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
